---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';
import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';
import {householdModel} from '../../dossiers/households.model.js';
import {householdChart,householdNumbers} from '../../dossiers/households.charts.js';
import {vintageText} from '../../../scripts/lib/vintage.mjs';
const D=bySlug.households,model=householdModel();
const groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))];
const period=model.plates.find(p=>p.bundle==='households-aidis-6').period,flowPeriod=model.plates.find(p=>p.bundle==='households-aidis-19').period;
const N=householdNumbers(model);
---
<Base title="Household assets and borrowing · India Atlas" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Households / <b>Assets and borrowing</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/households/" ogImage="/og/households.png">
<div class="page">
 <div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="households-aidis-6" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts">
  <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">Most of what an Indian family owns is land and the house it lives in. Most of what it owes is to a bank, though in villages a moneylender is still often the lender. A national survey of debt and investment counts both, household by household.</p></div>
  <div class="meta"><span>Assets and debt as on <b>{period}</b></span><span>Investment over <b>{flowPeriod}</b></span><span>Survey <b>All-India Debt and Investment Survey, NSS 77th round</b></span></div>
  <div class="numbers" data-long-values>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Average assets of a rural household</div><div class="v acc" id="n-rural_assets">{N["n-rural_assets"]}</div><div class="d">Land, buildings, animals, equipment and savings</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Average assets of an urban household</div><div class="v" id="n-urban_assets">{N["n-urban_assets"]}</div><div class="d">The same, for towns and cities</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Rural households owing money</div><div class="v" id="n-rural_incidence">{N["n-rural_incidence"]}</div><div class="d">Any outstanding cash loan</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Urban households owing money</div><div class="v" id="n-urban_incidence">{N["n-urban_incidence"]}</div><div class="d">Any outstanding cash loan</div></div>
  </div>
 </div>
 <section data-inputs="households-aidis-11, households-aidis-29" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>A family's balance sheet</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>A farming family in Bihar may own a small plot, a brick house, a buffalo and a bank account. On paper that can add up to several lakh rupees. Very little of it can pay a hospital bill next week: the land feeds the family and the house shelters it. Wealth in rural India is mostly of this kind, valuable and hard to turn into cash.</p><p>Debt works the other way. A crop loan from the bank, money from a relative for a wedding, an advance from the local moneylender before the harvest: each has its own price and its own pressure. Who lends, at what interest and for what purpose tells you more about a family's position than the amount alone.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>The All-India Debt and Investment Survey asks a sample of households across the country to list what they own and what they owe on one date, and what they spent on land, buildings and equipment over the year. It is the only national source that puts a household's assets and its debts side by side.</p><p>This round counted assets and debts as on {period}. The survey is run about once a decade, so it describes the country at that date. For bank lending since then, see the <a href="/guides/credit/">credit guide</a>.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <section data-inputs="all households-aidis bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read this survey</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>Averages include everyone.</b> Unless a chart says otherwise, an average is taken over all households, including those that own none of the asset or owe nothing. The average debt of a rural household is <b id="n-rural_debt">{N["n-rural_debt"]}</b>; a rural household that actually owes money owes <b id="n-rural_indebted">{N["n-rural_indebted"]}</b> on average.</p><p><b>Two dates, two kinds of figure.</b> Assets and outstanding loans are stocks counted on {period}. Spending on new houses, farm equipment and business assets is a flow over {flowPeriod}. The interviews came later, but they ask about those dates.</p><p><b>"Per 1,000" means two different things.</b> Some charts count households per 1,000 households; divide by ten for a percentage. Others split the money owed into rupees per ₹1,000 of debt, which divided by ten is a share of the debt. Each chart says which.</p><p><b>Deciles rank households by assets.</b> Decile 1 is the tenth of households with the least, decile 10 the tenth with the most. Rural and urban households are ranked separately.</p><p><b>One household, several answers.</b> A family can own land and a vehicle, and owe a bank and a relative. Ownership rates and borrowing rates by lender overlap and do not add up.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A survey estimate</span>The figures come from a large national sample of households. State figures and small groups rest on fewer households and carry more sampling error than the all-India numbers.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Valued by the household</span>Land and houses are valued at the prevailing local price, as the household reports it, not at a sale actually made.</aside></div></div>
 </section>
 <section id="current-reading" data-inputs="households-aidis-4, households-aidis-6, households-aidis-11, households-aidis-12, households-aidis-29, households-aidis-release" data-prose="data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What the survey found</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{period}</span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Land and buildings make up <b id="n-rural_land_building_pct">{N["n-rural_land_building_pct"]}</b> of what the average rural household owns, and <b id="n-urban_land_building_pct">{N["n-urban_land_building_pct"]}</b> in towns. Bank deposits are almost universal but small; shares barely register. Gold sits outside these totals: <b id="n-rural_gold">{N["n-rural_gold"]}</b> rural households and <b id="n-urban_gold">{N["n-urban_gold"]}</b> urban households keep some.</p><p>Wealth is very unequal. The tenth of rural households with the most assets average <b id="n-rural_top_decile">{N["n-rural_top_decile"]}</b>; the tenth with the least, <b id="n-rural_bottom_decile">{N["n-rural_bottom_decile"]}</b>. In towns the gap is wider: <b id="n-urban_top_decile">{N["n-urban_top_decile"]}</b> against <b id="n-urban_bottom_decile">{N["n-urban_bottom_decile"]}</b>.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Banks and other institutions hold <b id="n-urban_institutional_pct">{N["n-urban_institutional_pct"]}</b> of urban household debt but only <b id="n-rural_institutional_pct">{N["n-rural_institutional_pct"]}</b> in villages. Moneylenders hold <b id="n-rural_moneylender_pct">{N["n-rural_moneylender_pct"]}</b> of rural debt, against <b id="n-urban_moneylender_pct">{N["n-urban_moneylender_pct"]}</b> in towns.</p><p>An urban household owes <b id="n-urban_debt">{N["n-urban_debt"]}</b> on average across all households, and <b id="n-urban_indebted">{N["n-urban_indebted"]}</b> if it is one of those that borrow. Borrowing is more common in villages, but loans are larger in towns, where housing loans dominate.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 {groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={[...new Set(model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=>p.bundle))].join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound values"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  {model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.bundle}>
   <div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>{N['pv-'+p.id]}</div><div class="c">{p.rows[0].name}</div></div></div>
   <div class="frame"><div class="plotzone" id={'plot-'+p.id} set:html={householdChart(p,model)} /><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">MoSPI, All-India Debt and Investment Survey, {p.period}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div>
   <div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p></div></div>
  </figure></div>)}
 </section>)}
 <section data-inputs="all households-aidis bundles" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
 <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"The average household owes this much."</b> The average divides total debt by every household, most of which owe nothing. A family that borrows owes several times that average.</p><p><b>"Asset-rich means comfortable."</b> A rural family's wealth is mostly land and a house. It cannot sell either without losing its livelihood or its home, so a large asset figure can sit beside a cash shortage.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"Moneylenders are a thing of the past."</b> In villages they still hold a substantial share of the money households owe.</p><p><b>"A woman-headed household's assets are what women own."</b> The survey classifies households by their head. What women own in their own names is a different question it does not answer here.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <EveryCut slug="households"/><GuideData slug="households"/>
 <section data-inputs="all households-aidis bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources and method</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>Every figure comes from MoSPI's All-India Debt and Investment Survey (NSS 77th round, January to December 2019), as served by its eSankhyiki API, with the rural and urban estimates kept apart. The charts above answer one question each; every published table and cut, state by state, is in the table above and the downloads. The official report has further tables the API does not serve, including sampling errors and the number of households behind each estimate.</p><p><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/Report_no588-AIDIS-77R-SeptFinal_0.pdf">MoSPI: the AIDIS report, with definitions and detailed tables</a>. Data revision date: <b id="prov-vintage">{vintageText(model.plates[0])}</b>; MoSPI does not publish one for the API.</p></section>
</div>
<script>import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>
